      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "Prevent OpenCode reset timestamp overflow",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- reject non-finite numeric values at the OpenCode and OpenCode Go parser boundary\n- bound computed reset intervals before the trapping `Double`-to-`Int` conversion\n- preserve valid usage windows while treating malformed reset timestamps as having no reset\n- cover both infinite (`\"1e309\"`) and large finite (`\"1e308\"`) server values in both provider variants\n\n## After-fix behavior proof\n\nI ran the freshly built production parsers with the exact malformed server values through the focused executable test bundle. Both OpenCode variants now return the valid usage percentages and `resetInSec == 0` instead of terminating:\n\n```text\nOpenCodeUsageParserTests\n  \"ignores reset timestamps outside integer range\"\n  resetAt → \"1e309\" passed\n  resetAt → \"1e308\" passed\n  14 tests passed\n\nOpenCodeGoUsageParserTests\n  \"ignores reset timestamps outside integer range\"\n  resetAt → \"1e309\" passed\n  resetAt → \"1e308\" passed\n  28 tests passed\n```\n\nThe production path now checks the computed `Date.timeIntervalSince(now)` for finiteness and requires it to be strictly below `Double(Int.max)` before calling `Int(interval)`. Past timestamps still preserve the existing `0` behavior.\n\nA live account/provider probe is intentionally not used here: repository policy says parser behavior should use focused fixtures and explicitly prohibits live provider/browser-cookie probes unless requested. These fixtures exercise the crash boundary directly without reading cookies or Keychain data.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `swift test --disable-sandbox --filter OpenCodeUsageParserTests`\n- `swift test --disable-sandbox --filter OpenCodeGoUsageParserTests`\n- `make check` (0 formatting or lint violations)\n- `make test` (unrelated existing environment failure in `KeychainNoUIQueryTests`: `LAContext.interactionNotAllowed` remains false; focused suites pass)\n",
